    "text": "The salt air clung to Elias\u2019s skin like a second layer of wool, a constant reminder of the decades he had spent in the iron cage of the lighthouse. At seventy-two, his joints creaked in harmony with the groaning metal of the tower. He was the last of the keepers, a relic in a world of automated sensors and satellite navigation.\n\nOne Tuesday, the mail boat arrived with a single, yellowed envelope. The postmark was faded, but the date was unmistakable: *October 14, 1974*.\n\nElias frowned. He had been the keeper here for thirty years. Before him, it had been his father. Before that, his grandfather. No one had missed a mail run in half a century. The letter was addressed simply to *The Keeper, Blackrock Point*.\n\nHis hands trembled as he broke the wax seal. Inside was a single sheet of paper, the ink brown and brittle.\n\n*Dear Keeper,*\n\n*If you are reading this, the storm has finally broken. I am sorry I could not stay to see the dawn. I left the light burning for you, not for the ships, but for the thing that follows them. Do not look at the water after midnight. It is not the sea that is dark, but the eyes beneath it. Keep the light on. Keep it on until the sun rises. If the light goes out, they will know you are alone.*\n\n*\u2014 Arthur*\n\nElias dropped the paper. Arthur had been the keeper before his grandfather. Arthur had vanished in 1974 during a hurricane, his body never found. The official report said he had fallen overboard.\n\nA cold dread, colder than the Atlantic, settled in Elias\u2019s stomach. He looked at the clock. It was 11:58 PM. The storm had been raging for three days, a wall of black rain and howling wind that had isolated the island completely. The automated backup generator had failed an hour ago. The main light was flickering, sputtering on its last reserves of oil.\n\nElias rushed to the lamp room. The great Fresnel lens was dim, barely a whisper of gold against the encroaching night. He scrambled up the spiral stairs, his heart hammering against his ribs. He grabbed the manual crank.\n\n*Keep the light on.*\n\nHe cranked the mechanism, the gears grinding with a shriek that echoed in his bones. The light",
